P1 · S8 · 0035 — Pinned state holder (web-hook parity) — EXECUTION LOG
======================================================================

GOAL
Port the web `usePinned` hook domain (web/src/api/hooks/usePinned.ts, 3 hooks) to a
platform-agnostic, UI-free shared state holder in apps/shared/core, consumed identically by
Android/Apple (KMP) and Windows (C# port). Same endpoints, same snake_case params, same query
keys/caching intent, same invalidate granularity, SI preserved.

=== PARITY ===
usePinned.ts → 3 hooks, ALL ported:

READS (1):
  usePinned(type, context?)        → PinnedStore.pinned(type, context)
      GET /pinned?type=<wire>[&context=<context>], staleTime SLOW (5 min) → CacheDomain.Pinned 5 min.
      buildQuery faithfully reproduced by pinnedQuery(): `type` always sent; `context` sent
      whenever != null (explicit "" IS on the wire). Cache/feed key reproduced by
      pinnedCacheKey() = "<wire>:<null|v:context>", mirroring pinnedKeys.list = ['pinned', type,
      context ?? null]; the `v:` prefix keeps ABSENT(null) distinct from PRESENT "" / "null"
      exactly as the JS array tuples differ. One shared StateFlow<Resource<List<PinnedItem>>> per
      (type,context) bucket; same bucket ⇒ same feed, distinct bucket ⇒ distinct feed.

MUTATIONS (2):
  useTogglePin(type)               → PinnedStore.togglePin(type, itemId, pin, context?)
      pin=true:  POST /pinned {item_type, item_id[, context]} (context present-when-non-null).
      pin=false: resolve existing row id from the cached bucket (repo.peekPinned — the
                 getQueryData analogue) with a fresh-fetch fallback (repo.fetchPinned — the
                 `await request(...)` analogue) on a cold cache OR a cached-but-missing item;
                 then DELETE /pinned/{existing.id}. No matching row ⇒ Result.success(null) (web
                 returns null) which STILL refreshes. A failed fallback fetch / POST / DELETE is
                 propagated and refreshes nothing (web onError skips invalidation).
      onSuccess: refreshAll() — bumps EVERY observed feed = invalidateQueries(pinnedKeys.all)=['pinned'].
  useReorderPin(type)              → PinnedStore.reorderPin(type, id, position)
      PATCH /pinned/{id} {position}.
      onSuccess: refreshType(type) — bumps ONLY the (type, no-context) feed =
      invalidateQueries(pinnedKeys.list(type)) = ['pinned', type, null]. TanStack partial-match
      touches ONLY the null-context query (a ['pinned', type, 'ctx'] feed has 'ctx' != null at
      index 2, so it does NOT match) — reproduced exactly; verified by a rubber-duck critique.

SI / UNITS
Pin rows are ids / item ids / integer positions / RFC3339 timestamps — NOT unit-bearing — so they
round-trip verbatim as typed PinnedItem JSON with no SI conversion; display formatting is the
render boundary's job (S5). New cache domain Pinned = 5 min (matches web STALE_TIMES.SLOW).

DERIVATIONS GOLDEN-LOCKED (KMP+C# parity, PinnedGoldenTest, ADR-004):
  - pinnedQuery   (type always; context only when != null incl. explicit "")
  - pinnedCacheKey(web pinnedKeys.list null-vs-present distinction via the `v:` prefix; the
    'literal_null_string' vector proves null and the string "null" do NOT collide)

ENDPOINTS VERIFIED against internal/api/router.go (Route "/pinned": Get "/", Post "/",
Patch "/{id}", Delete "/{id}") and the Go handler internal/api/pinned/handler.go (List requires
?type=, optional ?context=; Create body item_type/item_id/optional context; Update body position;
Delete by id → 204) and the model internal/models/dashboard/pinned.go (closed PinnedItemType enum,
snake_case JSON tags). PinnedItemType modelled as a closed @Serializable enum with @SerialName
wire tokens (vehicle, widget, alert_rule, location, geofence, automation, dashboard, command).

ARCHITECTURE NOTES
Mutations have NO cache interaction in the repository (unlike the Annotations port's clear()):
invalidateQueries is refetch-not-remove, so the durable cache is left intact and the store's
targeted refresh (trigger bump → cacheThenNetwork always hits the network) re-fetches while the
last-known rows stay visible — a closer analogue of TanStack invalidation than eviction. The
repository makes the only network calls; the store injects the repo + scope and holds no Ktor.

